# Types { .heading-no-number }

---

## Structs

A `struct` is a product type with named fields:

```koka
struct person
  name : string
  age  : int

fun example-person()
  val p = Person("Alice", 30)
  println(p.name ++ " is " ++ p.age.show)
```

Structs are immutable. Update with a functional copy:

```koka
fun birthday( p : person ) : person
  p(age = p.age + 1)
```

---

## Sum Types

Use `type` for tagged unions (algebraic data types):

```koka
type color
  Red
  Green
  Blue

type maybe-int
  Nothing-int
  Just-int( value : int )
```

Koka has a built-in polymorphic `maybe<a>`:

```unchecked
type maybe<a>
  Nothing
  Just( value : a )
```

---

## Pattern Matching

Deconstruct values with `match`:

```koka
fun show-color( c : color ) : string
  match c
    Red   -> "red"
    Green -> "green"
    Blue  -> "blue"

fun example-color()
  println(show-color(Red))
```

Patterns can be nested and use guards:

```unchecked
type shape
  Circle( radius : float64 )
  Rect( w : float64, h : float64 )

fun area( s : shape ) : float64
  match s
    Circle(r) -> 3.14159 * r * r
    Rect(w, h) -> w * h

fun example-area()
  println(area(Circle(5.0)).show)
```

The compiler checks exhaustiveness -- missing cases are a compile error.

---

## Recursive Types

```koka
type tree<a>
  Leaf
  Node( left : tree<a>, value : a, right : tree<a> )

fun tree/show( t : tree<int> ) : string
  match t
    Leaf -> "."
    Node(l, v, r) -> "(" ++ l.show ++ " " ++ v.show ++ " " ++ r.show ++ ")"

fun example-tree()
  val t = Node(Node(Leaf, 1, Leaf), 2, Node(Leaf, 3, Leaf))
  println(t.show)
```

---

## Type Inference

Koka infers types pervasively. You can annotate when you want clarity or
when inference needs help (e.g., with overloaded functions):

```koka
fun double( x : int ) : int    // explicit
  x + x

fun triple( x : int )          // return type inferred
  x + x + x

fun example-infer()
  println(double(5).show)
  println(triple(5).show)
```

